Given an array nums of size n, return the majority element.
The majority element is the element that appears more than ⌊n / 2⌋ times. You may assume that the majority element always exists in the array.

Constraints:
n == nums.length
1 <= n <= 5 * 104
-109 <= nums[i] <= 109
The input is generated such that a majority element will exist in the array.
solve the problem in linear time and in O(1) space? 


class Solution {
    public int majorityElement(int[] nums) {

        // BOYER–MOORE VOTING ALGORITHM
        // This algorithm finds the majority element in O(n) time and O(1) space.
        // Assumption: A majority element (appearing more than n/2 times) always exists.

        int candidate = 0;  // Stores the current candidate for majority element
        int count = 0;      // Vote counter for the candidate

        // Single pass through the array
        for (int num : nums) {

            // If count is zero, select the current element as new candidate
            if (count == 0) {
                candidate = num;
            }

            // If current element supports the candidate, increment count
            // Otherwise, decrement count (pairwise cancellation)
            if (num == candidate) {
                count++;
            } else {
                count--;
            }
        }

        // Since the problem guarantees the existence of a majority element,
        // the final candidate is the majority element
        return candidate;
    }
}
